1
00:00:05,529 --> 00:00:07,580
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,584 --> 00:00:12,620
This time, let's look at the anatomy of the torso and the simplified drawing process.

3
00:00:12,621 --> 00:00:17,420
This time, another boring anatomy lesson has arrived.

4
00:00:17,421 --> 00:00:25,980
There are tons of muscles in the torso, but for the sake of my classmates and my own mental health, we'll focus on the essential big muscles only.

5
00:00:30,295 --> 00:00:34,560
For the neck, pay attention to a muscle called the sternocleidomastoid and the trapezius.

6
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:37,840
They're responsible for the silhouette of the neck.

7
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:41,401
It's one of the most visible muscles because it isn't covered by clothing.

8
00:00:42,005 --> 00:00:43,280
As you can tell from the name.

9
00:00:44,525 --> 00:00:50,360
It connects to the mastoid process, the sternum, and the clavicle.

10
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:54,001
It moves according to the movement of the head, so you need to express it well.

11
00:00:54,445 --> 00:01:00,700
The trapezius spans across the shoulder blade and the collarbone.

12
00:01:01,875 --> 00:01:06,581
It's a muscle that covers a fairly large area of the back and neck.

13
00:01:08,354 --> 00:01:13,390
It's good to draw it curving inward on the front side.

14
00:01:13,391 --> 00:01:16,110
Then there's the pectoralis major.

15
00:01:18,039 --> 00:01:19,930
It covers the ribcage broadly.

16
00:01:20,924 --> 00:01:24,570
It attaches twisted, over toward the upper arm bone.

17
00:01:26,229 --> 00:01:30,721
In a drawing, along with the ribcage, it's responsible for the thickness of the body.

18
00:01:31,574 --> 00:01:40,080
And on the side, you can see the latissimus dorsi, and if you look at it from behind, you can see it covers a very large area.

19
00:01:40,081 --> 00:01:42,640
Compared to its width, it's a relatively thin muscle.

20
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:47,040
Since it's responsible for the silhouette of the body, you could call it a very important muscle.

21
00:01:48,164 --> 00:01:54,480
The abs have little volume, so we'll draw them broadly and not cover them in this class.

22
00:01:54,481 --> 00:01:58,841
Grasp them as one big mass and one flow.

23
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:04,881
The external oblique muscle bulges out above the iliac crest of the pelvis.

24
00:02:04,924 --> 00:02:09,320
If you render this muscle well, it's great for a realistic sense of reliability.

25
00:02:10,835 --> 00:02:15,340
Body proportions differ from person to person, so I don't calculate them as I draw.

26
00:02:15,341 --> 00:02:18,420
For reference, I've divided the body into head-lengths.

27
00:02:18,919 --> 00:02:20,510
It's 8.5 heads tall.

28
00:02:20,511 --> 00:02:22,030
Please take that just as a reference.

29
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:33,520
Based on what we've learned so far, let's interpret the anatomy simply and draw it.

30
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:36,880
First, I drew the head simply.

31
00:02:36,881 --> 00:02:39,360
Then I'm going to draw the ribcage.

32
00:02:39,361 --> 00:02:44,640
You can think of the height of this ribcage as about one and a half times the head.

33
00:02:45,015 --> 00:02:48,841
And there's a necktie-shaped sternum in the middle.

34
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:55,230
Starting from the sternum, the clavicle goes up, curling backward in a V shape.

35
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:00,990
It joins the acromion on the shoulder blade.

36
00:03:05,429 --> 00:03:10,120
Above, there's the shoulder blade.

37
00:03:10,121 --> 00:03:14,881
The shoulder blade should come about halfway across the ribcage.

38
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:25,651
And here there's something called the mastoid process, a bump located behind the jaw and below the ear.

39
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:30,471
If you draw the necktie shape, it's positioned around here.

40
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,640
You can see it sticking out, right?

41
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:37,320
This point and the collarbone.

42
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:45,571
Spanning the sternum, I'll draw the sternocleidomastoid.

43
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:55,960
You just connect it like this.

44
00:03:55,961 --> 00:03:57,880
It's the muscle you see like this here.

45
00:04:00,250 --> 00:04:01,340
You can see it from above too.

46
00:04:06,674 --> 00:04:08,140
And then there's the trapezius.

47
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:25,500
When I draw a figure, I connect the body and head like this.

48
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:32,460
Rather than drawing the neck itself, I prioritize the silhouette of the trapezius and the levator scapulae.

49
00:04:32,461 --> 00:04:34,460
Let me draw the side view too.

50
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:38,300
I'll draw the collarbone, shoulder blade, and ribcage.

51
00:04:50,830 --> 00:05:00,350
Then, spanning the mastoid process, the sternum, and the clavicle, I'll draw the sternocleidomastoid.

52
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:17,770
Behind it, there's the trapezius.

53
00:05:17,771 --> 00:05:19,530
Let's keep drawing.

54
00:05:54,390 --> 00:06:01,040
Since this sternocleidomastoid attaches to the mastoid process of the head, it moves naturally along with the head.

55
00:06:05,215 --> 00:06:09,290
This part is thin, and this part is thick.

56
00:06:26,850 --> 00:06:27,900
Keep that in mind.

57
00:06:27,901 --> 00:06:32,860
Next, the line connecting the collarbone and the spine of the shoulder blade is important.

58
00:06:42,115 --> 00:06:46,190
Passing through the collarbone and acromion here.

59
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:54,860
If you draw the line connecting to the spine of the shoulder blade, it comes out rounded like this.

60
00:07:04,125 --> 00:07:09,160
Combining this line and the trapezius creates the silhouette around the neck.

61
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:31,930
Let me draw one more.

62
00:07:31,931 --> 00:07:35,570
The neck area can be simplified like this.

63
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:57,560
Now let's attach the pectoralis major.

64
00:07:57,561 --> 00:08:03,000
The pectoralis major starts around halfway along the collarbone and attaches to the arm bone.

65
00:08:06,565 --> 00:08:12,880
Draw it as if the upper arm bone is thickly covered on top.

66
00:08:15,610 --> 00:08:17,660
Flat but thick.

67
00:09:02,065 --> 00:09:07,580
Then I'll draw the silhouette of the latissimus dorsi, which I said is responsible for the silhouette.

68
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:13,511
This latissimus dorsi is also a muscle connected to the arm.

69
00:09:21,150 --> 00:09:29,150
I draw the latissimus dorsi on the near side.

70
00:09:29,151 --> 00:09:33,310
And you don't necessarily have to draw it, but let's check the abdominal cross-section line.

71
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:40,190
Then I'll draw the abdomen.

72
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,070
The latissimus dorsi on the opposite side probably isn't visible.

73
00:09:47,790 --> 00:09:55,790
It's visible from the front.

74
00:09:55,870 --> 00:10:00,030
This abdomen can change flexibly according to the movement of the spine.

75
00:10:11,315 --> 00:10:18,261
Also, you can draw a line slanting down from where the pectoralis major starts on the collarbone to express the abdomen.

76
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:20,890
That becomes the silhouette of the abs.

77
00:10:22,339 --> 00:10:24,010
Let me express it like this.

78
00:11:12,639 --> 00:11:17,440
And slightly below the narrowest part of the waist is the navel.

79
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:22,890
Above that, six abs will fit.

80
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:33,800
Like this.

81
00:11:33,801 --> 00:11:36,440
Please check the latissimus dorsi line again too.

82
00:11:49,185 --> 00:11:52,140
Now I'll express the external oblique.

83
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:58,800
I'll mark the iliac crest of the pelvis.

84
00:11:58,801 --> 00:12:01,120
And connect it naturally.

85
00:12:06,015 --> 00:12:09,731
This external oblique can create the waistline.

86
00:12:28,735 --> 00:12:31,290
Let's do the whole process again.

87
00:12:32,015 --> 00:12:33,050
Draw the head.

88
00:12:35,190 --> 00:12:39,320
Then the clavicle and trapezius.

89
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:45,950
Draw the sternocleidomastoid.

90
00:12:49,764 --> 00:12:53,790
Also render the hollow inside the neck.

91
00:12:53,791 --> 00:12:57,431
Create the thickness of the body with the pectoralis major.

92
00:13:00,705 --> 00:13:03,740
Set the silhouette of the body with the latissimus dorsi.

93
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:13,651
And draw the abdominal line influenced by the ribcage.

94
00:13:14,590 --> 00:13:19,081
What to watch here is that the abdomen isn't cylindrical.

95
00:13:22,430 --> 00:13:26,281
It's an oval shape closer to a rectangle.

96
00:13:30,935 --> 00:13:36,201
Mark the abdominal line and check the iliac crest.

97
00:13:50,289 --> 00:13:51,290
Then draw the external oblique.

98
00:13:51,291 --> 00:13:55,331
Let's indicate the angle of the arm using the skeleton too.

99
00:14:03,045 --> 00:14:08,320
When drawing the torso, you can sketch it simply just like when drawing the head.

100
00:14:08,321 --> 00:14:10,720
Let's look at the drawing video.

101
00:14:10,721 --> 00:14:15,840
I'll draw the sternocleidomastoid, the levator scapulae, and the collarbone of the neck we described.

102
00:14:15,841 --> 00:14:19,120
Then draw the lines of the pectoralis major and the latissimus dorsi.

103
00:14:19,121 --> 00:14:23,840
Then just draw the pelvis and external oblique lines.

104
00:14:23,841 --> 00:14:26,240
There aren't many things to draw.

105
00:14:26,241 --> 00:14:29,200
Let's draw it simply and joyfully.

106
00:14:29,255 --> 00:14:32,841
It's important to draw the torso in a variety of positions.

107
00:15:46,090 --> 00:15:47,930
The center of gravity is important.

108
00:15:47,931 --> 00:15:52,490
If the weight presses down on one shoulder, the legs have to support it.

109
00:15:52,491 --> 00:15:55,850
In that process, the waist naturally bends.

110
00:15:55,930 --> 00:15:59,130
It's easy to remember if you note that the pelvis rises on the side where the shoulder drops.

111
00:17:16,629 --> 00:17:24,920
In a standing pose, when you draw the center line of the body down to the floor, the point where it touches the floor should land on both feet or not go outside of them.

112
00:17:28,999 --> 00:17:32,840
When you draw the body, you should draw while thinking about the spine line.

113
00:17:32,841 --> 00:17:36,440
The spine line should follow the center of gravity that matches various poses.

114
00:17:37,134 --> 00:17:40,440
Along with the leg line that holds the center of gravity, of course.

115
00:17:40,441 --> 00:17:44,280
At this time, you should draw while reminding yourself that the spine is toward the back of our body.

116
00:17:45,719 --> 00:17:50,570
When drawing a woman, you need to reduce the size of the muscles and create beautiful lines.

117
00:17:50,570 --> 00:17:53,050
Especially, the silhouette of the ribcage is important.

118
00:17:53,214 --> 00:17:55,530
Pay attention to the blue line.

119
00:17:55,531 --> 00:17:59,610
Many people overlook the ribcage while trying to draw the chest nicely.

120
00:17:59,611 --> 00:18:05,770
Because this line doesn't show up well on men, the contrast gives it appeal.

121
00:18:05,850 --> 00:18:08,330
It's good to lift the shoulders a bit higher.

122
00:18:09,629 --> 00:18:14,240
Compared to a man, the waist should have a wider range of motion.

123
00:18:14,241 --> 00:18:23,360
Even with the same model, the feel differs depending on the pose, so if you do commercial art, you should focus on finding beautiful lines and poses.

124
00:18:23,361 --> 00:18:27,840
When drawing the chest, you need to vary its shape depending on the clothing the character will wear.

125
00:18:29,984 --> 00:18:35,660
It could be compressed, or it could be naturally relaxed.

126
00:18:35,661 --> 00:18:38,981
That's one of the reasons you shouldn't draw pictures by memorizing them.

127
00:18:42,814 --> 00:18:47,290
When drawing the back view, it's easier to draw the shoulder blades first.

128
00:18:47,291 --> 00:18:50,851
Because there are many muscles connected to the shoulder blades.

129
00:18:50,889 --> 00:18:57,050
The rendering of the latissimus dorsi, which is responsible for the lines of the back, will be important too.

130
00:18:57,051 --> 00:19:02,410
And along the spine line, the erector spinae muscles standing vertical are important, I think.

131
00:19:04,539 --> 00:19:09,230
Well-developed erector spinae add appeal.

132
00:19:09,231 --> 00:19:15,751
They're buried in the latissimus dorsi, but since the latissimus dorsi is a relatively thin muscle, they show on the surface.

133
00:19:20,749 --> 00:19:23,870
Unlike the face, the body is all about the silhouette.

134
00:19:24,594 --> 00:19:27,591
Let's practice with silhouette-focused drawing.

135
00:22:11,389 --> 00:22:15,710
This time, we looked at the anatomy of the body and simplified drawing.

136
00:22:15,710 --> 00:22:20,990
Next time, let's look at the anatomy of the arms and legs and simplified drawing.

